<% content_for :page_title, "Push notifications" %>
<%
  heading_class = "mb-2 mt-6 font-semibold"
  list_class = "list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300"
%>

<h1 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Push notifications</h1>

<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
  Waterhole can tell you when registrations are waiting, even with no Waterhole page open.
  It's off until you turn it on, in each browser you want it in.
</p>

<h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">Turning it on</h2>
<ul class="<%= list_class %>">
  <li><strong>iPhone and iPad:</strong> Apple only allows notifications for sites on the home screen.
    In Safari, tap Share, then “Add to Home Screen”. Open Waterhole from that new icon, sign in there
    (it doesn't share Safari's sign-in), and turn notifications on from the account menu.</li>
  <li><strong>Android and desktop browsers:</strong> go to Notifications in the account menu and choose
    “Turn on in this browser”. On Android, adding Waterhole to the home screen is optional.</li>
  <li><strong>When your browser asks,</strong> choose Allow. If you blocked it earlier, allow notifications
    for this site in your browser's settings first.</li>
  <li>Then use <strong>Send a test notification</strong> to check that it works. Also check your device's
    Do Not Disturb or Focus settings, which can hide notifications.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">What gets sent, and through whom</h2>
<ul class="<%= list_class %>">
  <li>Only a count, such as “2 new registrations are waiting”. Never an applicant's details, and not the instance's name.</li>
  <li>Messages travel through the push service of your browser's vendor: Apple, Google, Mozilla or Microsoft.
    The message is encrypted, but the vendor can see that your browser received one, and when.</li>
  <li>Waterhole does not check whether you're looking at the queue, so you may get a notification about
    something already on your screen.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">Good to know</h2>
<ul class="<%= list_class %>">
  <li>Registrations are fetched every five minutes, so even “every 5 minutes” can lag a little.</li>
  <li>“Every 5 minutes” and “every hour” can be limited to the hours you choose, in your time zone.
    Nothing is lost: the first notification once they begin counts everything that arrived since the day's last one.</li>
  <li>Signing out does <strong>not</strong> stop notifications, because sessions expire within a week and
    you would stop hearing from Waterhole. On a shared device, turn them off or remove the browser from the list.</li>
  <li>If notifications stop arriving, open the Notifications page in that browser and turn them on again:
    browsers sometimes replace or drop their push address.</li>
  <li>Notifications pause while you can't use Waterhole: when your instance sign-in has lapsed, or when the
    privacy policy changed and you haven't agreed to it yet. No notification tells you this has happened.
    Once you've signed in or agreed, they pick up where they left off.</li>
</ul>
